Abstract—This paper presents AI MedLab, an integrated medical laboratory system leveraging artificial intelligence for disease prediction and health recommendations. The system combines a web-based frontend, a Python backend (Flask), and machine learning models (XGBoost, scikit-learn) to deliver accurate, real-time predictions and personalized reports. Evaluation demonstrates high accuracy and practical workflow integration for healthcare professionals.

Index Terms—Artificial Intelligence, Medical Laboratory, Disease Prediction, Healthcare, Machine Learning, Web Application

I. INTRODUCTION
The rapid advancement of artificial intelligence (AI) has enabled new possibilities in healthcare, particularly in automating diagnosis and supporting medical decision-making. AI MedLab is designed to streamline patient management, disease prediction, and health recommendations through a user-friendly web interface and robust backend infrastructure. The motivation for this work is to address the need for efficient, accurate, and scalable diagnostic tools in medical laboratories, reducing manual workload and improving patient outcomes.

II. SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE AND METHODOLOGY
A. System Overview
AI MedLab consists of a frontend (HTML5, CSS3, JavaScript) and a backend (Python Flask REST API). The frontend provides role-based dashboards for patients, doctors, and nurses, supporting login, registration, data entry, and report download. The backend manages authentication, prediction, health recommendations, and data storage, with CORS enabled for secure communication.

B. Component Details
Frontend files include index.html (login/registration), dashboard.html (main dashboard), script.js (client logic), and style.css (styling). Backend files include app.py (main application), routes/auth.py (authentication), routes/predict.py (prediction), config/db.py (database), and model/ (ML models and data).

C. Machine Learning Model
The backend integrates an XGBoost classifier trained on a healthcare dataset with 22 features (age, gender, 20 symptoms). Gender is encoded numerically, and disease labels are mapped using scikit-learn’s LabelEncoder. The model achieves over 95% accuracy in multi-class disease classification (11 diseases). Model training uses an 80-20 train-test split, and evaluation metrics include accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score.

D. Workflow
Users input data via the frontend, which is sent to the backend for processing. The backend predicts disease, generates health recommendations, and creates PDF reports using ReportLab. The workflow supports real-time feedback and documentation for healthcare professionals.

III. RESULTS AND EVALUATION
A. Evaluation Metrics
The system is evaluated using accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score. Test results show high performance, with accuracy exceeding 95%. Evaluation scripts (test_api.py, test_model_predictions.py) cover API endpoints, model accuracy, and data flow.

B. Demo Workflow
The workflow enables seamless data entry, prediction, and report generation. Users interact with the dashboard, submit patient data, receive predictions and recommendations, and download PDF reports. The system supports role-based access and real-time updates.

IV. DISCUSSION
AI MedLab demonstrates the potential of AI in medical laboratories, offering efficient, accurate, and scalable solutions. Limitations include dataset scope, real-world integration challenges, and the need for broader disease coverage. Future work will focus on expanding disease coverage, improving user experience, and integrating with electronic health records.

V. CONCLUSION
AI MedLab provides a practical AI-driven platform for medical laboratories, enhancing diagnostic accuracy and workflow efficiency. The system’s modular design and high performance make it suitable for deployment in diverse healthcare settings. The integration of machine learning and web technologies streamlines diagnostic processes and supports healthcare professionals.

VI. FIGURES
Figure 1. System Architecture Diagram
The system architecture consists of a web browser frontend (client interface), communicating via HTTP with a Python http.server (port 8000) and a Flask REST API backend (port 5000). The backend connects to model storage (XGBoost model, encoders, feature data) and data storage (users.json, patient history, predictions, reports). PDF generation is handled by ReportLab. The diagram illustrates the flow of data from user input to prediction and report generation.

